(Title) Tj
T*
(Safety, tolerability, and preliminary efficacy of biomechanical foot interventions in) Tj
T*
(individuals with knee osteoarthritis and foot pain: a pilot randomized feasibility study) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Biomechanical interventions targeting the lower extremity can reduce knee) Tj
T*
(joint loading; however, effects on concurrent foot symptoms are unclear. This pilot) Tj
T*
(feasibility study examined angled foot inserts with and without arch support in patients) Tj
T*
(with both knee osteoarthritis and foot pain. Methods: 30 adults with radiographically-) Tj
T*
(confirmed knee osteoarthritis and foot pain were randomized to receive either 6-degree) Tj
T*
(angled inserts alone \(n=15\) or angled inserts integrated with contoured foot and arch) Tj
T*
(support \(n=15\) for 12 weeks. Feasibility measures included recruitment/retention rates,) Tj
T*
(daily wear compliance, comfort ratings, and adverse event frequencies. Clinical measures) Tj
T*
(included foot function indices, knee symptoms questionnaires, and motion capture analysis) Tj
T*
(of lower limb biomechanics. Results: All feasibility criteria were successfully met with) Tj
T*
(no serious adverse events reported. Participants rated insert comfort as moderate \(mean) Tj
T*
(7.0/10\), and 83% of weeks met the target 30-minute daily wear threshold. Both groups) Tj
T*
(showed consistent improvements in knee pain and function scores, knee adduction moment) Tj
T*
(reduction, and ankle stability measures. No statistical comparisons were conducted due to) Tj
T*
(pilot design. Conclusions: Both insert types appear safe and feasible in this population;) Tj
T*
(larger confirmation studies are warranted.) Tj
